\begin{abstract}
This paper introduces a mathematical framework that models particle worldlines as four-dimensional (4D) superhelical filaments within a discretized hypersphere lattice. We replace point-particle abstractions with a mechanical model where physical properties emerge from the geometric interaction between 4D worldline trajectories and a propagating 3D intersection surface. We define a Master Lagrangian governing the dynamics of these recursive filamentary structures and a Universal Indicatrix mapping function to project these 4D paths into 3D observables. This framework operates on a zero-parameter economy, where all physical constants and mass hierarchies derive from a single dimensional anchor and the specific geometry of a 24-cell lattice architecture.
