\section{Introduction}

The standard representation of matter as point-particles in a vacuum requires the manual insertion of numerous independent constants. In contrast, this framework proposes a four-dimensional geometric foundation where "particles" are recontextualized as persistent, superhelical worldline filaments. These filaments exist within a discretized 4D hypersphere lattice that expands radially at the rate of $c$.

The 3D "present moment" is modeled as a propagation surface (or timesheet) that intersects these filaments. The superhelical coiling of the worldline is not a fanciful ornamentation but a structural model of internal degrees of freedom. Winding and linking numbers—quantified as the topological charge $Q$—determine the mechanical drag, or resistance, that a filament encounters as the 3D surface moves along the time-flow vector. This geometric metaphor provides an intuitive mechanical basis for inertia and mass while remaining ontologically neutral regarding the underlying substance of the filaments.

