\subsection{Anchoring and Scaling}
Anchoring is the procedure of fixing a primary scale (e.g., $l_f$) and propagating it through all physical sectors. For example, the Universal Mass Anchor $m_0 \approx 1.0073 \times 10^{-27}$ kg is derived directly from the filament tension and scale. Scaling for different particle types then follows strict geometric ratios, such as the projection constant $B \approx 0.2387$ rad, which represents the per-dimension share of distortion during 4D to 3D mapping.

